::GEO_U03_PT_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 46° and 59°. What is the third angle?
{
~A. 85°#This does not make the three angles total 180°. Recheck the Triangle Angle Sum Theorem.
=B. 75°#Correct. Triangle interior angles sum to 180°, so 180 - 46 - 59 = 75.
~C. 105°#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle. Subtract the known angles from 180°.
~D. 65°#This subtracts incorrectly. The known angles sum to 105°, leaving 75°.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle of a triangle is 128°. One remote interior angle is 53°. What is the other remote interior angle?
{
~A. 181°#This adds the exterior angle and the remote interior angle. The Exterior Angle Theorem says the remote interior angles add to the exterior angle.
~B. 65°#This would make the remote interior angles sum to 118°, not 128°.
~C. 52°#This confuses the exterior angle relationship with a linear-pair subtraction. Use 53 + x = 128.
=D. 75°#Correct. By the Exterior Angle Theorem, 53 + x = 128, so x = 75.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which triangle congruence criterion uses two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle?
{
=A. SAS#Correct. SAS uses two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle.
~B. SSS#SSS uses three pairs of corresponding sides. It does not include an angle.
~C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side. The question gives two sides and an included angle.
~D. AAA#AAA does not prove triangle congruence because triangles can have the same angles but different sizes.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which information is not enough to prove two triangles congruent?
{
~A. Three pairs of corresponding sides#Three pairs of corresponding sides support SSS, which proves triangle congruence.
~B. Two angles and the included side#Two angles and the included side support ASA, which proves triangle congruence.
=C. Three pairs of corresponding angles#Correct. AAA can show the same shape, but it does not guarantee the same size, so it does not prove congruence.
~D. Hypotenuse and one leg in right triangles#Hypotenuse-leg information supports HL for right triangles.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Why can valid triangle congruence criteria be connected to rigid transformations?
{
~A. They allow one triangle to be stretched until it matches the other.#Stretching changes distance, so it is not a rigid transformation and does not justify congruence.
=B. They give enough evidence to map one triangle exactly onto the other without changing size or shape.#Correct. Valid congruence criteria support a rigid-motion match because rigid transformations preserve distance and angle measure.
~C. They depend only on the triangles looking the same.#Appearance is not proof. Congruence criteria use mathematical evidence, not visual guessing.
~D. They prove only that the triangles have equal areas.#Equal area alone does not prove triangle congruence. Congruence requires same size and shape.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
A reflection followed by a translation maps triangle ABC exactly onto triangle DEF. What conclusion is supported?
{
=A. Triangle ABC ≅ triangle DEF#Correct. A sequence of rigid transformations that maps one triangle exactly onto another supports triangle congruence.
~B. The triangles are similar but not congruent.#Reflections and translations are rigid transformations, so they preserve exact size and shape.
~C. The triangles must be different sizes.#Rigid transformations preserve size, so this conclusion contradicts the evidence.
~D. No conclusion can be made.#A rigid-motion mapping is valid evidence for congruence, so a conclusion can be made.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, what is true about opposite sides?
{
~A. They are always perpendicular.#Perpendicular opposite sides are not a general parallelogram property.
~B. They are always different lengths.#Opposite sides in a parallelogram are congruent, not always different.
~C. They have no relationship.#Parallelograms have defined opposite-side relationships.
=D. They are congruent.#Correct. Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
The diagonals of a parallelogram intersect at M. If AM = 6 on diagonal AC, what is MC?
{
~A. 3#This halves AM, but M is already the midpoint of diagonal AC.
~B. 12#This is the full diagonal AC, not the other half MC.
=C. 6#Correct. Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so AM = MC.
~D. 18#This triples the known half and does not use the diagonal bisection property.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
A trapezoid has bases 10 and 18. What is the length of its midsegment?
{
=A. 14#Correct. The trapezoid midsegment length is the average of the bases: (10 + 18) / 2 = 14.
~B. 8#This subtracts the base lengths. The midsegment uses the average, not the difference.
~C. 28#This adds the bases but does not divide by 2.
~D. 180#This multiplies the bases. The trapezoid midsegment formula does not use multiplication.
}
::GEO_U03_PT_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
In an isosceles trapezoid, one base angle measures 72°. What is the measure of the other angle on the same base?
{
~A. 108°#This may describe an adjacent supplementary angle, but the question asks for the other angle on the same base.
~B. 36°#This halves the angle without a supporting trapezoid relationship.
~C. 144°#This doubles the angle without using the isosceles trapezoid base-angle property.
=D. 72°#Correct. Base angles on the same base of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent.
}
